The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!fsnotify_get_mark_safe(iter_info->marks[type])) {
    __release(&fsnotify_mark_srcu);
    goto fail;
}
```
Reason:
This unconditionally fails (releases SRCU and jumps to `fail`) when `fsnotify_get_mark_safe()` returns false for any mark, even if the mark belongs to an unrelated group (not `iter_info->current_group`). Since `fsnotify_get_mark_safe()` can return false for a mark from a different group (e.g., during that mark’s removal), the permission event is incorrectly bypassed, causing a false positive.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
while (mark && !fsnotify_get_mark_safe(mark)) {
    if (mark->group == iter_info->current_group) {
        __release(&fsnotify_mark_srcu);
        goto fail;
    }
    /* This is a mark in an unrelated group, skip */
    mark = fsnotify_next_mark(mark);
    iter_info->marks[type] = mark;
}
```
And also the removal of the early return in `fsnotify_get_mark_safe()`:
```
-	if (!mark)
-		return true;
```
Reason:
The new `while` loop handles the case where a mark cannot be pinned by `fsnotify_get_mark_safe()`. If the mark belongs to the current group, it fails (as before) because that mark is essential for the permission check. If the mark is from an unrelated group, it simply skips to the next mark in the list, avoiding an unnecessary failure. The removal of the NULL check in `fsnotify_get_mark_safe()` is safe because the loop condition already handles NULL marks (the loop is not entered). This fix ensures that permission events are not bypassed when a mark in an unrelated group cannot be pinned.

